## Damage and repair realism
- Lasting damage between battles (lasting_damage.md): knocked-out equipment stays offline until port repair (tested)
- Repair rework (repair_rework.md, v0.17.11): new damage control colours (yellow / orange / red / black / grey), engine rooms repairable at sea to 50% health and half power (tested)
- Possible follow-up: count patched (half-power) engine rooms in the campaign map speed too
- **Bomb over-penetration (planned, v0.17.13) (M):** a bomb can (1) hit the deck and explode there, (2) dud, (3) punch through into the ship and explode inside for more damage, or (4) over-penetrate: go straight through a lightly built ship and explode underneath or in the water, doing much less damage (but it can leave a hole that floods).
  - Checked in the game code: possible. The game already handles 1-3. A bomb's armour piercing (`mmArmorPen` in the ammunition file, x a random 0.88-1.12 from `bombPenetrateRange`) is compared with the deck armour in `DamageableSea.ApplyDamage`. If it doesn't get through, it does 20% damage (`bombNonPenetrateDamage`), less on big ships. If it does, it does double damage and can carry on to the deck below. What's missing is option 4: nothing makes a bomb pass right through. It would go in `DamageableSea.ApplyDamage`. When the bomb's piercing is far above the armour (an armour-piercing bomb hitting a destroyer, for example) there's a chance it passes through, with the damage cut and a flooding chance. Hit log result: "passed through". Tag `[BOMBPEN]`.
  - Dud rate: **already in the game.** `dudRateBombByNation` in config.txt (9% for every nation except one at 3%), only active when the game's "dud bombs" option is on (otherwise bombs never dud). Duds already show "BombHitDud" in the message log. It can be tuned in config.txt without code, or we can add a per-bomb dud rate (for example armour-piercing bombs dud more) if wanted.
  - Torpedo dud rates (v0.17.13, data only, no code): every torpedo already has its own `dudRate` in its ammunition file. Change them to historical 1942 values: Mark 14 0.35 -> 0.6, Mark 15 0.3 -> 0.6, Mark 13 0.3 -> 0.5, Mark 18 0.06 -> 0.1, Type 91 0.04 -> 0.03, Type 93 / 95 / 96 0.04 -> 0.05, 8th Year / 6th Year 0.04 -> 0.06 (Mark IX 0.06 and Mark 24 0.05 unchanged). Torpedoes only dud when the game's "dud torpedoes" option is on. Possible follow-up (code): US torpedoes get better as the campaign date passes their real fixes (Mark 14 / 15 exploder September 1943, Mark 13 mid-1944).
  - **Torpedo hit angle (planned, v0.17.13) (M):** the angle a torpedo hits at changes what happens. Checked in the game code: possible. A torpedo hitting a ship goes through `Ammunition.OnTriggerEnter`, then the dud roll in `Ammunition.ExplodeAmmunition`, and both the torpedo's and the ship's headings are known there, so the hit angle can be worked out and the dud chance changed before the roll.
    - Glancing hits (under about 20-25 degrees to the hull): a chance the torpedo glances off without exploding, for every nation. Hit log: "glanced off".
    - Historical twist for US torpedoes with the early war contact pistol (Mark 14, Mark 15, Mark 13): in real life a near perfect 90 degree hit was the *most* likely to dud, because the firing pin got crushed before it could fire (70% failed in the 1943 cliff tests), while glancing hits often worked. So for these the dud chance goes up near 90 degrees and down at angles.
    - First version: a glancing torpedo just stops (like a dud). Making it carry on running after the glance is harder and can come later.
    - Tag `[TORPHIT]`.

